mp;w=96"/></div><div class="_1jiz7rn2 _1jiz7rn3"><span class="_1jiz7rn6">Dominic Preston</span><span class="duet--article--timestamp g4xafw2 g4xafw0 _19wv7tc5 _1jiz7rna"><time dateTime="2026-09-04T17:11:42+00:00">Sep 4</time></span></div></div><div class="_4ine551 _4ine550"><div class="_4ine554">Insta360’s Luna Pro shoots 4K vertical video.</div><div class="_1y5ugvbf _1y5ugvb0"><p class="duet--article--dangerously-set-cms-markup duet--article--standard-paragraph _1044qizi _18mzr4b1 _18mzr4b0 _19wv7tc1">The Luna Pro is already out in China. We’re still waiting for a US launch, but it was on the company’s stand at IFA so I had a play. It’s single-lens, but can use the 8K sensor to capture 4K footage either landscape or portrait, and has the <a href="/tech/947218/insta360-luna-ultra-dual-camera-stabilized-8k-telephoto-leica-us-availability-pricing">Ultra</a>’s detachable display too.</p></div></div><div class="_1p001ee0"><div class="_1y5ugvbj"><div style="position:relative"><div><section tabindex="-1" aria-label="carousel" class="duet--article--gallery _1044qizj xnv72h0"><div class="_1044qizx xnv72h1" id=":R2l776drar96:"><div class="xnv72h3"><a class="xnv72h2" href="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/insta360-luna-pro-3.jpg?quality=90&amp;strip=all" data-pswp-height="2456" data-pswp-width="3684" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img alt="&lt;em&gt;The Luna Pro looks a lot like the Ultra, with similar controls.&lt;/em&gt;" data-chromatic="ignore" loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-nimg="fill" class="xnv72h7 xnv72h5 xnv72h4 i7ks070" style="position:absolute;height:100%;width:100%;left:0;top:0;right:0;bottom:0;color:transparent;background-size:cover;background-position:50% 50%;background-repeat:no-repeat;background-image:url(&quot;data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg xmlns=&#x27;http://www.w3.org/2000/svg&#x27; %3E%3Cfilter id=&#x27;b&#x27; color-interpolation-filters=&#x27;sRGB&#x27;%3E%3CfeGaussianBlur stdDeviation=&#x27;20&#x27;/%3E%3